  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \n\nThe Ohio Valley continues to remain under the influence of surface\nhigh pressure to start the week. The closed upper low along the \nAtlantic Coast becomes absorbed back into the mean H5 flow and gets \ncarried off. This feature was originally causing a blocking pattern, \nkeeping similar forecast conditions across the ILN fa the past few\ndays. However, as the low gets carried off, subtle H5 ridging \ndevelops over us and will result in a warming trend through the work \nweek. Majority of our CWA will have highs back in the 80s by \nWednesday. \n\nGlobal models and ensembles have continued to show a signal for a\nwetter end to the week as an H5 trough and associated low track \nthrough the central CONUS, eventually ushering some of that moisture \nour way. However, there has been a slower trend in the arrival of \nprecip chances, with Thursday now trending drier during the daytime \nhours. Rain chances gradually increase as we progress into the \nweekend with the eastward propagation of the low.",
